syntax: zsh's short forms of loops with an empty body

Unless SHORT_LOOPS is unset, zsh lets the body of "while", "until",
"for", "select" and "repeat" be a single sublist instead of "do list
done", and that sublist may also be empty. zsh takes the list after
"while" or "until" up to the first token that cannot continue it as the
condition, and after the header of the other loops it skips any number
of separators; whatever token then cannot start a command, including the
end of the input or of a substitution, ends the loop. zsh accepts and
runs, with both commands as the condition and no body:

	{ while a; b; }
	while false; print x
	for i in 1;
	repeat 2
	echo $(for i (1 2))
	foreach i (1); while a; end

The words after "in" still need a ";" or a newline after them, so
"for i in 1" at the end of the input is an error, as is "for i in 1;;"
in a case item. An empty body directly followed by "&", "&!" or "&|"
is an error too, and so is "end" anywhere but at the end of a
"foreach", since zsh reserves it. Names after "for" stop at a word which
closes a compound command, as in "{ for i }".

Reproducer, before this change:

	$ printf '{ while a; b; }\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:3: `while <cond>` must be followed by `do`

With LangZsh, a loop whose header is not followed by "do" or "{" now
takes the short form, with Do empty unless a sublist follows, and the
End of WhileClause, ForClause and RepeatClause falls back to the end of
the header. "end" closes a compound command like "fi" or "done" does.
The printer needs no change: an empty body prints as "do; done", which
zsh accepts.

Part of https://github.com/mvdan/sh/issues/120.

